  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms today, some of which \nmay be strong with gusty winds and small hail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest KCBW radar and satellite data shows two areas of showers\nand thunderstorms. First area of storms are along a cold front\nthat is quickly moving through Northern Aroostook County\nextending back to Moosehead Lake region. There is a secondary\narea of storms extending from near Bangor and this is convection\ndeveloping along and just northwest of the marine layer\nboundary. Temperatures in the 70s and low 80s between these two\nboundary is allowing for SBCAPE to be around 1500j/kg, 18z CAR \nRAOB indicating 2048j/kg of SBCAPE. There is 25-30kt of\nindicated deep layer shear thanks to recent VWP scans. 18z CAR\nRAOB confirms this thinking but given how fast this cold front\nis moving storms are moving too fast to fully organize despite\nthe fairly modest mid-level lapse rates. Cannot rule out a few\nthunderstorms that make it to severe levels but much of the\nactivity will be sub-severe with gusty winds and perhaps small\nhail but the FZL is around 13kft. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...System bringing rain showers and chance for \nthunder moves in Sunday afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system in Canada moves eastward this weekend, with\ntrough axis moving through Sunday afternoon. Cold front moves \nthrough the state Sunday evening, funneling in some cooler air \nbehind it. Rain moves into the north, generally expanding \nsoutheastward during the day Sunday. PWATs generally around \n1-1.50 inches based on global and regional model outputs. Global\nmodels are keeping precipitation chances pretty modest, around \n14 of an inch, while regional models are hinting at some more \nconvective storms developing Sunday evening, bumping up rain \ntotals to one half to three quarters of an inch in isolated \nlocations, predominantly in the north and southeast. Instability\nparameters include few hundred joules of CAPE, 40-50 kts of \neffective shear, and some decent low level lapse rates, \nindicating that a thunderstorm is not out of the question. \nHeating ahead of the cold front in eastern Maine could aid in \ninitiating some convection along the boundary. Confidence is \nstill low for any severe thunderstorms to develop, given the \nmodel disagreement, but regional models are hinting at better \ninstability parameters than earlier indicated.",
